      "description": "Passed priority instead of using Polluted Delta (for revolt) + Fatal Push to kill Yawgmoth, Thran Physician. Yawgmoth was the only non-summoning-sick creature that could block Emperor of Bones. Removing it would deal 3 damage AND eliminate the opponent's key combo/engine piece.",
      "action_taken": "Passed priority, allowing Yawgmoth to block Emperor of Bones.",
      "better_line": "Activate Polluted Delta to fetch a land (enabling revolt), then cast Fatal Push targeting Yawgmoth, Thran Physician. Emperor of Bones attacks unblocked for 3, and the opponent loses their most important creature.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 323,
      "player": "Qwen3L Teach",
      "decision_index": 127,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "The player's reasoning was incorrect - Emperor of Bones is 3/3 and Yawgmoth deals only 2 damage, so Emperor survives at 3/1. While Yawgmoth also survives, it's left at 2/1 making it very vulnerable. Blocking with Young Wolf would have been better: Young Wolf dies (triggering undying, returning as a 2/2), deals 1 damage to Emperor, and Yawgmoth stays at full 2/4 health.",
      "action_taken": "Blocked Emperor of Bones with Yawgmoth, Thran Physician, leaving Yawgmoth at 2/1 and Emperor alive at 3/1",
      "better_line": "Block with Young Wolf - it dies and returns with undying as a 2/2 (free value), Emperor takes 1 damage, and Yawgmoth stays at full toughness. Alternatively, use Yawgmoth's activated ability to sacrifice Young Wolf for value before/after blocking decisions.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 324,
      "player": "MiniMx Rules",
      "decision_index": 128,
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "Passing the turn without casting Thoughtseize (castable off Blood Crypt for {B}) wastes an opportunity to strip a key card from the opponent's hand and gain critical information. The opponent has Yawgmoth, Thran Physician on board which is a powerful engine, and seeing their hand could reveal threats or answers. Additionally, Ragavan could have been cast for {R} off Blood Crypt to provide an additional attacker next turn, applying pressure while the opponent is at 9 life. Passing with 2 mana available and castable spells is a significant waste of tempo.",
      "action_taken": "Passed priority without casting any spells, leaving Blood Crypt and Polluted Delta untapped with Thoughtseize and Ragavan both castable.",
      "better_line": "Cast Thoughtseize targeting the opponent to strip a key card (like Walking Ballista or any combo piece they might draw), then potentially also deploy Ragavan off the remaining mana via Polluted Delta fetching a Mountain-type land, or at minimum cast Thoughtseize to gain information and disrupt the opponent's plans.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 330,
      "player": "Qwen3L Teach",
      "decision_index": 129,
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "Activated Spymaster's Vault to connive X where X = 0 (no creatures died this turn), which does literally nothing. This wastes the Vault's tap and {B} from another source, reducing available mana for casting Walking Ballista. With 6 lands untapped, they could cast Ballista at X=3 (a 3/3), but wasting mana on a do-nothing activation reduces the Ballista's potential size.",
      "action_taken": "Activated Spymaster's Vault for connive 0, accomplishing nothing",
      "better_line": "Cast Walking Ballista for X=3 using all 6 available mana, getting a 3/3 that can pressure the opponent or remove Psychic Frog",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 332,
      "player": "MiniMx Rules",
      "decision_index": 130,
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "Cast Walking Ballista from hand with X=0 (all lands tapped), which enters as a 0/0 and immediately dies to state-based actions, accomplishing nothing meaningful. The reasoning mentions wanting to kill Psychic Frog, but the wrong Walking Ballista was selected.",
      "action_taken": "Cast Walking Ballista from hand for X=0, resulting in it dying immediately as a 0/0",
      "better_line": "Activate the Walking Ballista already on the battlefield (id=p116) to remove +1/+1 counters and deal damage to Psychic Frog (2 damage kills it), or use Yawgmoth to sacrifice Young Wolf targeting Psychic Frog with -1/-1 counter while drawing a card (Young Wolf returns with undying for repeated use).",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 342,
      "player": "MiniMx Rules",
      "decision_index": 132,
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "Casting Walking Ballista for X=0 means it enters as a 0/0 and immediately dies to state-based actions, wasting the card for nothing. All lands are tapped so X cannot be anything other than 0.",
      "action_taken": "Cast Walking Ballista for X=0, which dies immediately as a 0/0",
      "better_line": "Activate Yawgmoth, Thran Physician by sacrificing Young Wolf to draw a card and put a -1/-1 counter on Emperor of Bones (reducing it to 2/2). Young Wolf returns with undying as a 2/2. This generates card advantage and shrinks the opponent's threat while preserving the Walking Ballista for later.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 346,
      "player": "MiniMx Rules",
      "decision_index": 134,
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "Psychic Frog is a 1/2 blocking Yawgmoth, a 2/4. Psychic Frog deals 1 damage to Yawgmoth (doesn't kill it) and takes 2 damage, dying in the process. The Frog doesn't 'remove' Yawgmoth at all — it just trades itself for 2 damage prevention. Meanwhile, Psychic Frog is summoning sick so it can't attack this turn, but it's a valuable card advantage engine that could be used on future turns. Blocking Yawgmoth with the Frog sacrifices the Frog for merely 2 damage prevention (taking 1 from Young Wolf instead of 3 total). The player's reasoning that 'Psychic Frog will survive' is completely wrong — a 1/2 does not survive combat with a 2/4. Additionally, the opponent has Yawgmoth which can sacrifice creatures and draw cards, and killing their own Psychic Frog in combat gives the opponent nothing, but losing this card advantage engine is costly. A better option would have been to not block at all (take 3 damage, going from 15 to 12), or use Fatal Push on Yawgmoth (the more threatening creature) while blocking Young Wolf with Psychic Frog (surviving that block as a 1/2 vs 1/1). Actually, Fatal Push on Yawgmoth requires revolt for mana value 4, and no permanent left the battlefield this turn under Qwen3L's control yet — but Polluted Delta could be cracked to enable revolt, then Fatal Push kills Yawgmoth.",
      "action_taken": "Blocked Yawgmoth (2/4) with Psychic Frog (1/2), which kills the Frog while Yawgmoth survives.",
      "better_line": "Crack Polluted Delta to enable revolt, then cast Fatal Push targeting Yawgmoth (mana value 4, killed with revolt). Block Young Wolf with Psychic Frog (1/2 survives blocking 1/1). This removes Yawgmoth permanently while preserving Psychic Frog.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 354,
      "player": "Qwen3L Teach",
      "decision_index": 136,
    },
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "Failed to recognize that both Yawgmoth and Walking Ballista have activated abilities that don't require mana. Could have used Yawgmoth to sacrifice Young Wolf (undying returns it), sacrifice it again, putting two -1/-1 counters on Emperor of Bones, drawing 2 cards, then pinging Emperor with Walking Ballista to kill it. This eliminates the opponent's most dangerous recursive threat.",
      "action_taken": "Passed the turn, reasoning incorrectly that no mana meant no actions available.",
      "better_line": "Activate Yawgmoth (pay 1 life, sac Young Wolf, -1/-1 on Emperor, draw). Young Wolf returns with +1/+1. Activate Yawgmoth again (pay 1 life, sac Young Wolf, -1/-1 on Emperor, draw). Then remove a +1/+1 counter from Walking Ballista to deal 1 damage to Emperor of Bones, killing it. Net result: kill Emperor, draw 2 cards, cost 2 life.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 360,
      "player": "MiniMx Rules",
      "decision_index": 139,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Discarded Ragavan, Nimble Pilferer instead of Polluted Delta. Ragavan is a high-impact 1-mana creature that can be cast immediately next turn off Blood Crypt for early pressure and value generation (treasure + exile cards), while Polluted Delta is just a land drop that's less critical when they already have 3 lands in play.",
      "action_taken": "Discarded Archon of Cruelty and Ragavan, Nimble Pilferer",
      "better_line": "Discard Archon of Cruelty and Polluted Delta, keeping Ragavan as a cheap threat to deploy next turn alongside Thoughtseize or Fatal Push",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 368,
      "player": "Qwen3L Teach",
      "decision_index": 143,
    },
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "The opponent has the Yawgmoth + Walking Ballista (2 +1/+1 counters) + Young Wolf infinite combo assembled on board. When they untap next turn, they can sacrifice Young Wolf to Yawgmoth repeatedly, putting -1/-1 counters on Ballista (which removes +1/+1 counters), drawing their deck and draining with Ballista pings. This is a lethal combo.",
      "action_taken": "Passed priority without taking any action, allowing the opponent to untap with the combo intact.",
      "better_line": "Activate Polluted Delta to fetch a Swamp (getting {B}), then cast Fatal Push targeting Walking Ballista (MV 0, easily destroyed). This breaks the infinite combo. Could also play the second Polluted Delta from hand first as the land drop for additional mana later.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 369,
      "player": "Qwen3L Teach",
      "decision_index": 144,
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "With Yawgmoth and Young Wolf on the battlefield, the player has access to a repeatable draw engine (sacrifice Young Wolf to Yawgmoth, it returns via undying with a +1/+1 counter, put -1/-1 counter on it to cancel, repeat). At 9 life with opponent fully tapped out, they could draw ~4-7 cards before committing mana to spells, finding much better options than a 2/2 Walking Ballista.",
      "action_taken": "Planning to cast Walking Ballista for X=2 without first using the Yawgmoth + Young Wolf draw engine.",
      "better_line": "Activate Yawgmoth by sacrificing Young Wolf repeatedly (paying 1 life each time, putting -1/-1 counters on Young Wolf when it returns with undying to reset it) to draw multiple cards first, then decide how to best spend mana based on what was drawn.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 389,
      "player": "MiniMx Rules",
      "decision_index": 152,
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "Failed to use Yawgmoth's sac ability and Walking Ballista to clear Emperor of Bones before damage, missing 3 additional combat damage plus a card draw. Could sac Young Wolf to Yawgmoth (-1/-1 on Emperor, draw a card, Young Wolf returns via undying), then use both Ballista counters to kill the weakened Emperor, letting Yawgmoth swing unblocked for 4 instead of trading with Emperor while only Young Wolf's 1 damage gets through.",
      "action_taken": "Passed without activating either Walking Ballista or Yawgmoth, allowing Emperor of Bones to block Yawgmoth and only dealing 1 damage with Young Wolf.",
      "better_line": "Activate Yawgmoth sacrificing Young Wolf (pay 1 life, -1/-1 on Emperor making it 2/2, draw a card; Young Wolf returns with undying as 2/2), then remove both +1/+1 counters from Walking Ballista to deal 2 damage to Emperor killing it, letting Yawgmoth deal 4 unblocked combat damage (opponent to 9 instead of 12).",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 401,
      "player": "MiniMx Rules",
      "decision_index": 155,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Chose to earthbend Boseiju, Who Endures instead of a basic land. While earthbend does return the land when it dies, animating Boseiju unnecessarily exposes a unique utility land (Channel ability) to creature removal, temporarily losing access to it. A basic Forest would provide identical mana value with Badgermole Cub's bonus (G+G) while being completely replaceable if temporarily removed.",
      "action_taken": "Earthbended Boseiju, Who Endures, making it a 1/1 creature-land",
      "better_line": "Earthbend a basic Forest instead, preserving Boseiju's availability for its Channel ability while getting the same mana benefit from Badgermole Cub's creature-mana bonus",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 411,
      "player": "MiniMx Rules",
      "decision_index": 158,
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "The player activated Walking Ballista (removing a +1/+1 counter to deal 1 damage) instead of using Yawgmoth's ability, which could sacrifice Young Wolf (triggering undying to return it with a +1/+1 counter), draw a card, and place a -1/-1 counter on Emperor of Bones to neutralize its +1/+1 counter. Emperor of Bones with no counters is much less threatening since Adapt becomes live again but the reanimation trigger is delayed. Drawing a card with an empty hand is extremely valuable.",
      "action_taken": "Activated Walking Ballista to deal 1 damage, shrinking it from 2/2 to 1/1 and accomplishing relatively little.",
      "better_line": "Activate Yawgmoth sacrificing Young Wolf, targeting Emperor of Bones with the -1/-1 counter. This draws a card (critical with empty hand), Young Wolf returns via undying with a +1/+1 counter, and Emperor loses its +1/+1 counter. Then could potentially chain further Yawgmoth activations or use Ballista shots more strategically.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 413,
      "player": "MiniMx Rules",
      "decision_index": 159,
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "Emperor of Bones exiles a card from a graveyard, and when a creature card is exiled this way, it can later put that creature onto the battlefield with haste (and a finality counter). Exiling Unearth (a sorcery) does nothing useful - it can't be put onto the battlefield by Emperor of Bones' second triggered ability, and it's just removed from the graveyard for no benefit. The player should have exiled a high-value creature card like Archon of Cruelty from their own graveyard, which would allow Emperor of Bones to put it onto the battlefield with haste when +1/+1 counters are placed on Emperor (e.g., via Adapt). Archon of Cruelty entering would force the opponent to sacrifice a creature, discard, lose 3 life, while drawing a card and gaining 3 life - potentially game-winning at these life totals.",
      "action_taken": "Exiled Unearth (a sorcery) with Emperor of Bones' combat trigger, gaining no benefit since Emperor can only reanimate creature cards exiled with it.",
      "better_line": "Exile Archon of Cruelty from the graveyard, then use Emperor of Bones' Adapt ability ({1}{B}) to put +1/+1 counters on it, triggering the ability to put Archon of Cruelty onto the battlefield with haste. This would be devastating against the opponent at 9 life.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 419,
      "player": "Qwen3L Teach",
      "decision_index": 161,
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "Walking Ballista blocked Emperor of Bones (3/3) as a 2/2, dealing only 2 combat damage and failing to kill Emperor. The player could have removed one +1/+1 counter from Walking Ballista to ping Emperor for 1 damage during combat, then the remaining 1/1 Ballista would deal 1 combat damage for a total of 2 - still not enough. However, they could ping for 1 before damage then the 1/1 blocks dealing 1 combat = 2 total. Actually the correct line is to block with the 2/2 AND use the ping ability to remove a counter during combat (before or after damage) to deal 2 combat + 1 ping = 3 total damage, killing Emperor. The Ballista dies either way.",
      "action_taken": "Blocked Emperor of Bones with Walking Ballista (2/2), dealing only 2 damage to the 3/3 Emperor, leaving it alive.",
      "better_line": "Block with Walking Ballista and also activate its ability to remove one +1/+1 counter to ping Emperor of Bones for 1 additional damage (total 3), killing it. Ballista dies either way, so the ping is free value.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 425,
      "player": "MiniMx Rules",
      "decision_index": 163,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Should play Polluted Delta before casting Thoughtseize. Playing the land first costs nothing and provides an additional mana source. More importantly, if Thoughtseize reveals something that changes the plan, having Polluted Delta on the battlefield gives more flexibility. Additionally, cracking Polluted Delta later enables revolt for Fatal Push to kill creatures with MV 4 or less.",
      "action_taken": "Cast Thoughtseize without first playing Polluted Delta from hand",
      "better_line": "Play Polluted Delta first, then cast Thoughtseize. This preserves maximum flexibility and enables revolt for Fatal Push if needed later.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 431,
      "player": "Qwen3L Teach",
      "decision_index": 164,
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "Passed the turn with untapped mana and a land in hand. Failed to play Polluted Delta (free land drop) and failed to cast Unearth to permanently return Psychic Frog or Ragavan from graveyard, both of which are high-value creatures that would significantly impact the game.",
      "action_taken": "Passed priority without making any plays despite having available mana and impactful options.",
      "better_line": "Play Polluted Delta, then cast Unearth targeting Psychic Frog (or Ragavan) from the graveyard to establish a threatening creature on board.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 433,
      "player": "Qwen3L Teach",
      "decision_index": 165,
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "Casting a second legendary Yawgmoth, Thran Physician when one is already on the battlefield forces the legend rule to sacrifice one copy. This spends 4 mana ({2}{B}{B}) essentially to get a creature death trigger and a fresh untapped Yawgmoth, but the existing Yawgmoth could already be used with Young Wolf's undying to draw cards and put -1/-1 counters on the opponent's Emperor of Bones. The mana spent on this redundant legendary could have been used for Yawgmoth activations instead.",
      "action_taken": "Cast a second Yawgmoth, Thran Physician, triggering the legend rule and losing one copy immediately.",
      "better_line": "Use the existing Yawgmoth's activated abilities (sacrifice Young Wolf to draw cards and put -1/-1 counters on Emperor of Bones), leveraging the undying loop with the mana that would have been spent on the redundant legendary cast.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 435,
      "player": "MiniMx Rules",
      "decision_index": 166,
    },
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "Exiled Young Wolf (a 1/1) with Emperor of Bones instead of Archon of Cruelty from their own graveyard. With {1}{B} available to Adapt 2 the Emperor (which has no +1/+1 counters), they could reanimate the exiled creature. Archon of Cruelty's ETB would force opponent to sacrifice a creature, discard, lose 3 life, while drawing a card and gaining 3 life - a massive swing. Instead they get a 1/1 for one attack.",
      "action_taken": "Exiled Young Wolf from opponent's graveyard with Emperor of Bones' combat trigger",
      "better_line": "Exile Archon of Cruelty from own graveyard, then Adapt 2 Emperor of Bones for {1}{B} to put Archon onto the battlefield, triggering its devastating ETB (opponent sacs creature, discards, loses 3 life; you draw card, gain 3 life) and getting a 6/6 flying attacker.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 471,
      "player": "Qwen3L Teach",
      "decision_index": 178,
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "Chose Young Wolf (1/1) over Endurance (3/4) from Emperor of Bones exile trigger. Endurance would deal 2 more combat damage (bringing opponent closer to lethal) and its ETB could shuffle MiniMx Rules' stacked graveyard (containing Walking Ballista, Yawgmoth, Badgermole Cubs, etc.) to the bottom of their library, severely disrupting their graveyard-based strategy with Yawgmoth and Dredger's Insight.",
      "action_taken": "Put Young Wolf onto the battlefield - a 1/1 that attacks for 1 and provides no additional value since finality counter prevents undying.",
      "better_line": "Choose Endurance to get a 3/4 attacker and use its ETB to shuffle the opponent's graveyard away, denying future Unearth/recursion targets and weakening their Dredger's Insight value engine.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 481,
      "player": "Qwen3L Teach",
      "decision_index": 181,
    },
    {
      "severity": "questionable",
      "description": "Discarding Thoughtseize over a second Unearth is debatable. Thoughtseize costs 2 life (putting Qwen3L from 6 to 4), which is very costly at 6 life, but it provides crucial information and disruption against the opponent's draws. However, having two Unearths when there are already good reanimation targets in the graveyard (Archon of Cruelty, Psychic Frog, Ragavan) means the second Unearth has real value. The key consideration is that at 6 life, Thoughtseize's 2 life cost is very painful, making it less castable. Still, the information and disruption could be vital to survive.",
      "action_taken": "Discarded Thoughtseize",
      "better_line": "Discarding the second Unearth might be slightly better since you already have one copy and the Thoughtseize could provide critical disruption even at low life, but this is genuinely close given the life total consideration.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 497,
      "player": "Qwen3L Teach",
      "decision_index": 184,
    }
  ],
